<section class="how">
    <h2>How to Use This Morse Code Translator</h2>
    <p>Using this decoding tool is completely automatic and requires almost zero technical knowledge. You simply click into the top input box and begin typing your standard English sentence. As you type the letters the bottom box will instantly update generating the exact sequence of dots and dashes required to transmit your specific message.</p>
    <p>If you have received a secret message in Morse code you can use this same tool to uncover its meaning. Just paste the scattered dots and dashes directly into the top box. The system is smart enough to recognize that you are entering code instead of regular letters. It automatically switches into decoding mode and reveals the hidden English text instantly in the results area. Once you are finished converting your text you can tap the play button to actually hear your message transmitted through authentic audio beeps simulating classic telegraph radio.</p>
</section>

<section class="formula">
    <h2>Understanding the Morse Code Alphabet</h2>
    <p>Morse code was created in the nineteenth century to send information across long electrical telegraph wires. Because early machines could not send voice patterns they used electrical pulses of specific lengths. A short pulse is called a dot while a long pulse is called a dash. These two simple sounds combine in different sequences to represent every letter of the alphabet and all numbers between zero and nine.</p>
    <p>The code is specifically designed for speed and efficiency. The most commonly used letters in the English language require the shortest transmission sequences. For example the letter E is the most widely used character in English so its code is simply one single quick dot. Conversely an uncommon letter like Q requires a much longer complex sequence of two dashes a dot and another dash. When writing the code down on paper operators separate individual letters with one space and separate whole words with a slash mark.</p>
</section>

<section class="examples">
    <h2>Famous Morse Code Examples</h2>
    <p>The most famous code sequence in human history is undoubtedly the international distress signal SOS. Contrary to popular belief it does not actually stand for Save Our Ship. It was chosen purely because its rhythmic pattern is incredibly distinctive and practically impossible to misinterpret over radio static.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>SOS Distress Signal:</strong> <code>... --- ...</code></li>
        <li><strong>Hello World:</strong> <code>.... . .-.. .-.. --- / .-- --- .-. .-.. -..</code></li>
        <li><strong>I Love You:</strong> <code>.. / .-.. --- ...- . / -.-- --- ..-</code></li>
        <li><strong>Numbers 1 to 3:</strong> <code>.---- ..--- ...--</code></li>
    </ul>
</section>

